From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 16:15:19 -0500
Subject: fix: device pickers in a Space's own session; selected pins stay out
 of a collapsed folder; page buttons in the site panel (S-402, S-414, S-415,
 S-418, S-419)

Device pickers in a Space with an independent session (spaces B47; GitHub
issue 73): Chromium refuses Web Serial, WebUSB, WebHID, Web Bluetooth, the
File System Access pickers and on-device translation and speech to a web
page in any storage partition but the profile's default, because their
grants are the profile's and a <webview>'s partition must not share them. A
Space's jar is one of the profile's own, whose site permissions are the
profile's in every Space, so those checks count it as the default.

A collapse keeps the selected pins out (folders F3, settings T55; GitHub
issue 78): collapsing a folder keeps listed the pins selected in it, each
while awake, until the folder opens. "Keep open tabs out of a collapsed
folder", off by default and a row of the command bar, keeps every awake pin
listed instead. Peek's settings stay out of the command bar (commandbar K9),
so "Open links from other apps as a peek" has no row there.

The page's buttons in the site panel (site-controls Si15; GitHub issue 45):
while the window shows no Back button -- the sidebar's line too narrow for
the three, the toolbar hidden -- the strip starts with Back, Forward and
Reload.

Centred icons (sidebar Y51; GitHub issue 45): TopContainerButton centres its
image. Left aligned, a LabelButton puts it at its border's 6 DIP inset, which
centres only a 20 DIP icon in the 32 DIP button.

A new window steps 22 DIPs on Windows, as on the Mac (windows G11): with 10,
a second window looked as if it sat on the first.

Why: GitHub issues 73, 78 and 45, and the owner's test on the PC,
2026-11-04.
Removable when: never as behaviour; the partition checks when Chromium
scopes device grants to a storage partition (crbug.com/40068594).
